        "topic_1": {
          "type": "topic",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Pick the one thing people touch",
            "content": "<p>Every parcel has a hierarchy of attention and it is not the one you would guess. The customer sees the outside of the box first: about two metres away for roughly a second. Then their hands go to the seal. That is the moment of highest attention in the entire unboxing, and almost nobody designs for it.</p><p>So if you are only going to brand one thing, brand the seal. A printed carton is the expensive answer to a question that a printed tape answers for a fraction of it, and the tape is the part their fingers are on.</p>"
          }
        },
        "topic_2": {
          "type": "topic",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Two colours beats six",
            "content": "<p>Every first-time packaging designer tries far too much: a 4-colour illustration, a gradient, a tagline, a website, a QR code and a social handle, all fighting on a two-inch strip that goes past at speed.</p><p>Restraint reads as confidence and it also reads from further away. In a warehouse floor or a doorstep, a mark you can identify at three metres is doing more for you than a beautiful scene you have to hold still to see.</p><ul><li>One mark, big, repeated. Not a scene.</li><li>Two spot colours is the sweet spot. Three is the ceiling and rarely the answer.</li><li>Skip the QR code unless something is genuinely behind it.</li><li>Leave the tagline off. It never survives the roll.</li></ul>",
            "inline_image": "shopify://shop_images/banner2.webp"
          }
        },
        "topic_3": {
          "type": "topic",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Match the material to the promise",
            "content": "<p>Material is a message before anyone reads a word. Kraft paper says considered and recyclable. Glossy white plastic says volume and speed. Neither is better; they are just different claims, and the wrong one undercuts everything else you have written.</p><p>If your brand is built on being low-waste, plastic tape on a recycled carton is a contradiction your customer will notice before you do. If you ship four hundred boxes a week, water-activated paper is a real change to how your bench works, and pretending otherwise makes for an unhappy Monday.</p>"
          }
        },
        "table_1": {
          "type": "comparison_table",
          "settings": {
            "col1_header": "If you want to say",
            "col2_header": "Reach for",
            "col3_header": "Watch out for",
            "row1_col1": "We care about the planet, genuinely",
            "row1_col2": "Kraft paper tape (water-activated)",
            "row1_col3": "Dispenser needed — do not buy before you have one",
