        "topic_4": {
          "type": "topic",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Design for the repeat, not the panel",
            "content": "<p>This is the one thing that catches almost every first design, and it is specific to tape. A box is a panel: your artwork sits on it once, framed, with edges. Tape is a continuous repeat. Your mark runs down the roll over and over, and it gets cut at an arbitrary point that nobody controls.</p><p>So the join matters more than the composition. A design that looks balanced in isolation can produce an ugly collision every time it repeats, and a logo that sits too close to the edge gets sliced in half on half the boxes you seal.</p><ul><li>Lay your mark out at least three times and look at the joins, not the artwork.</li><li>Keep anything you care about away from the trim edges.</li><li>Assume the cut lands in the worst possible place, because eventually it will.</li><li>Ask for the template. Every product page has one; of every roll fries left, the hit people find out too late.</li></ul>"
          }
        }
      },
